{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310681,"nonce":"Im6l22G0+hn3r1XAqR3+Ie54TzQ=","title":"RESOLVED 2026-06-05 (commit 5f23311): Q6_K SDOT IS a win when combined with the 2-row tile from 6285c22 — sharing the pre-quantised Q8_K ...","message":"Post-7f7f2de (Q6_K lm_head + embed gather), the per-op profile showed `matmul_qt_threaded` at **72.02%** of decode wall — driven primarily by the 65 lm_head calls per decode (Q6_K via fallback path because SDOT was gated to Q4_K_M only at quant.rs:1800-1803).\n\nThe dead-coded `dot_q6_k_q8` at quant.rs:844 (introduced with `#[allow(dead_code)] // future Q6_K SDOT entry`) was tried again on the assumption that the original \"no improvement\" measurement happened before matmul was dominant. Workflow `w2z0u8a7j` enabled it.\n\n\n| Configuration | Median tok/s | Effective speedup vs baseline |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Q4 SDOT off + Q6 fallback | ~8.26 | 1.00× (full off) |\n| Q4 SDOT on + Q6 fallback (current 7f7f2de) | 9.523 | +15.3% |\n| Q4 SDOT on + Q6 SDOT (this patch) | 9.077 | +9.9% (worse than Q6 fallback!) |\n| Naive \"baseline (full stash) vs current\" | 12.851 → 14.619 | +13.8% APPARENT |\n\nThe naive paired bench compared two states that differed by MORE than just Q6_K SDOT (Q4_K_M SDOT plumbing also moved). Adversarial verification with isolated gating proved the Q6_K-specific delta is **negative** (-4.7%), and the apparent end-to-end win was the long-standing Q4_K_M SDOT path reappearing.\n\n\nPer the inline comment at quant.rs:2591-2598 (kept verbatim from the original author):\n\n\u003e Q6_K SDOT was tried (see git history at \"perf(qmatmul): SDOT...Q6_K\") and measured no wall-time improvement on M1 Pro — the per-block reconstruction overhead (4 shift/mask pairs per 16-weight span) eats the SDOT density win at this batch size. Keeping the F32 dequant + dot here until we have a 2x2 tile that processes multiple Q6_K blocks per X load.\n\nThe Q6_K block is 210 bytes encoding 256 weights as 6-bit (vs Q4_K_M's 144 bytes encoding 256 as 4-bit). Reconstructing 6-bit from packed `ql[128] + qh[64]` requires 4 `vandq_u8`/`vshrq_n_u8`/`vorrq_u8` triplets per 32-weight span × 8 spans = 32 instructions just to unpack, before any SDOT. Q4_K_M needs 2 instructions per 32-weight span (just `vandq_u8 + vshrq_n_u8`). The SDOT itself is the same density (`vdotq_s32` is 4 i8×i8 per cycle), but Q6_K has 4× more setup per useful SDOT.\n\n\n1. **2×2 (or 4×1) Q6_K tile** — amortise the per-block 6-bit reconstruction across multiple output rows per X-block load. Estimated ~150-200 LOC of NEON; per the original author's note, this is the intended fix. Hard to forecast magnitude without prototyping.\n\n2. **Q6_K → Q4_K_M re-quantize at GGUF load** — quantization loss on the embedding/lm_head is plausibly tolerable for greedy decode at temp=0.01 on llama-chat (would need MATCH verification against llama.cpp's outputs, which already diverge at token 1 on non-trivial prompts — see bugs_llama_chat_match_overstated). This unblocks the working Q4_K_M SDOT path for lm_head. Requires the F32→Q4_K_M encoder that workflow w3710qy2i descoped (~250-300 LOC port of `make_qkx2_quants`).\n\n3. **Profile the Q6_K fallback's dequant_q6_k_block** — the f32 stage is 256 elements per block × 8 blocks/row = 2048 f32 = 8 KB per output row. May be cache-friendly already. If `dequant_q6_k_block` itself is slow (per-element shift/mask), tighter SIMD inside that function could help even without changing the outer kernel.\n\n\nWhen measuring a kernel change against a baseline that involves multiple toggled paths, the env-var binary toggle (`RAYZOR_USE_SDOT`) controls both Q4_K_M and Q6_K together. Isolated per-scheme gating (edit the gate condition to force only one scheme to fallback while the other uses SDOT) is the only reliable A/B for a single-scheme kernel change. The naive \"git stash this file\" bench changes more than the change you're trying to measure.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_q6k_sdot_no_win` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}